<h1 class="entry-title">Tomb of Marshal Le Van Duyet (Lang Ong Ba Chieu)</h1>


</header>


</div>


</div>


</div>


<div class="page-spacer clearfix">


<div class="content-area" id="primary">


<div class="container">


<div class="row">


<main class="site-main col-xs-12 col-sm-8 left-block" id="main">


<article class="post hentry">


<a href="/en/blog-details.html">


</a>


<header class="entry-header">


</header>


<div class="entry-content">


<div class="post-frame">


<img alt="Post image" src="/images/llvd.png"/>


<div class="img-ref">Source: lyluanphebinh.vn</div>


</div>


<h3>Introductory Article</h3>


<h4>• Value</h4>


<p>


    Recognized as a national historical relic, the Tomb of Marshal Lê Văn Duyệt holds not only spiritual significance for the people of Saigon but also stands as an architectural and artistic masterpiece that reflects the talent and creative labor of generations of Southern Vietnamese. Having endured for more than 200 years with many ups and downs, the Thượng Công Temple remains a remarkable architectural work that embodies the essence of old Saigon — a distinctive cultural landmark of the city.


  </p>


<div class="post-frame">


<img alt="Post image" src="/images/llvd1.jpg"/>


<div class="img-ref">Source: laodong.vn</div>


</div>


<h4>• Origins</h4>


<p>


    The tomb was built in 1848 during the reign of Emperor Thiệu Trị to honor Marshal Lê Văn Duyệt — a renowned official of the Nguyễn Dynasty, known for his talent and dedication. <br/>


He not only helped defend the Gia Định region but also contributed to developing it into an important economic and cultural center. After his death in 1832, due to political conflicts, his tomb was destroyed during the reign of Emperor Minh Mạng. <br/>


Under Emperor Thiệu Trị, his titles and honors were restored, and the tomb complex was rebuilt into a solemn place of worship. Lăng Ông Bà Chiểu is not only the resting place of the Marshal but also a symbol of the people’s deep respect and gratitude toward a figure who made great contributions to the history of Southern Vietnam. <br/>


Over the centuries, the tomb has become an important historical and spiritual site, closely tied to the culture of Saigon.


  </p>


<div class="post-frame">


<img alt="Post image" src="/images/llvd2.jpg"/>


<div class="img-ref">Source: nld.com.vn</div>


</div>


<h4>• Explore Structural Values</h4>


<p>


    Lăng Ông covers an area of 18,501 m² on a high mound, located at the intersection of four streets: Lê Văn Duyệt, Phan Đăng Lưu, Trịnh Hoài Đức, and Vũ Tùng. <br/>


Lăng Ông Bà Chiểu features an ancient architectural style that harmoniously blends traditional Vietnamese design with Nguyễn Dynasty influences, creating a space that is both solemn and intimate. <br/>


The stele house within the complex preserves inscriptions honoring the great contributions of Marshal Lê Văn Duyệt. A large stone stele is placed inside a curved-roof pavilion decorated with dragon motifs, symbolizing majesty and reverence. Surrounding the stele house are lush green trees, offering visitors a peaceful atmosphere for reflection and remembrance. <br/>


The tombs of the Marshal and his wife lie at the center of the complex. The two graves are positioned side by side, enclosed by intricately carved walls. His tomb follows feng shui principles, paved with marble to represent harmony and endurance. The surrounding greenery enhances the serene and dignified ambiance. <br/>


The main temple serves as the spiritual heart of the site, with the central hall dedicated to Marshal Lê Văn Duyệt, adorned with gilded plaques and parallel sentences praising his merits. The middle hall venerates other historical figures such as Deputy Governor Lê Chất and Viceroy Phan Thanh Giản, while the western hall honors his wife and other revered ancestors. <br/>


In addition, a bronze statue of the Marshal stands prominently within the temple, capturing his commanding presence and unwavering devotion. <br/>


Through these features, the temple is not only a memorial site but also a sacred destination where locals place their faith and prayers for peace and well-being.


  </p>


<div class="post-frame">


<img alt="Post image" src="/images/llvd3.png"/>


<div class="img-ref">Source: saigoneer.com</div>


</div>


</div>


